    scenario A: deploy.sh pins the secret VERSION NUMBER, mints SA tokens with the service audience, fails run on HTTP errors, idempotent enable/pause; typed NULLs in the vector SQL
    
    Found by the adversarial review of the Pass-1 sequence before its first run:
    
    - `secret` and `deploy` read the Secret Manager version with `--format value(name)`, which is the full resource path
      (projects/N/secrets/hubspot-token/versions/V); `--set-secrets HUBSPOT_TOKEN=hubspot-token:<path>` would have been rejected.
      Now `value(name.basename())`, the version number.
    - `invoke` (dry, run, replay): when gcloud's active account is a service account (a Workbench instance, a VM) the ID token is
      minted with the service URL as audience, which Cloud Run requires; a person's gcloud token is used as is. `curl -sSf`, so an
      HTTP 403 (no run.invoker) or 409 (lease held) fails the step instead of passing a JSON error body as a payload; `run` also
      refuses a payload without rows_written.
    - `enable`/`pause` check the job state first: the Scheduler API refuses to resume a job that is not PAUSED, so a second
      `enable` used to exit non-zero. The header now says what is and is not a no-op (secret adds a version, run moves the watermark).
    - `check-env` refuses a HUBSPOT_TOKEN line in .env (the comment promised it, nothing enforced it).
    - tests/test_hot_score.py emit_sql: typed NULLs (CAST(NULL AS STRING|FLOAT64|BOOL)) and escaped backslashes. With bare NULLs a
      struct column that is NULL in every row of the UNNEST is typed INT64 and the SAFE_CASTs / STRING parameters fail — it cannot
      happen on the golden vectors, it does on live deals (the notebook's live-parity cell). tests/hot_score_vectors.sql regenerated.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
